1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,557
My topic is handedness-whether in different sports it is better to be left-

2
00:00:06,557 --> 00:00:11,650
or right-sided or whether a more balanced approach is more successful,

3
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:18,125
I'm left-handed myself and I actually didn't see any relevance to my own life

4
00:00:18,125 --> 00:00:23,037
when I happened to start reading an article by a sports psychologist called Peter

5
00:00:23,037 --> 00:00:24,200
Matthews.

6
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,761
He spent the first part of the article talking about handedness in music instead

7
00:00:29,761 --> 00:00:30,464
of sport,

8
00:00:30,464 --> 00:00:34,800
which I have to say almost put me off from reading further.

9
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:40,936
But what I soon became struck by was the sheer volume of both observation

10
00:00:40,936 --> 00:00:46,471
and investigation he had done in many different sports and I felt persuaded that

11
00:00:46,471 --> 00:00:50,060
what he had to say would be of real interest.

12
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:56,112
I think Matthews' findings will be beneficial, not so much in helping sportspeople to

13
00:00:56,112 --> 00:00:57,716
work on their weaker side,

14
00:00:57,716 --> 00:01:03,327
but more that they can help them identify the most suitable strategies to use

15
00:01:03,327 --> 00:01:04,630
in a given game.

16
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:11,955
Although most trainers know how important handedness is, at present they are rather reluctant

17
00:01:11,955 --> 00:01:16,450
to make use of the insights scientists like Matthews can give,

18
00:01:16,450 --> 00:01:23,386
which I think is rather short-sighted because focusing on individual flexibility is only part

19
00:01:23,386 --> 00:01:24,210
of the story.

20
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:27,130
Anyway, back to the article.

21
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:33,487
Matthews found a German study which looked at what he called 'mixed-handedness',

22
00:01:33,487 --> 00:01:38,420
that is, the capacity to use both left and right hands equally.

23
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:44,560
It looked at mixed-handedness in 40 musicians on a variety of instruments.

24
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:49,113
Researchers examined a number of variables, e.g.

25
00:01:49,113 --> 00:01:57,080
type of instrument played, regularity of practice undertaken and length of time playing instrument...

26
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,302
and found the following:

27
00:01:59,302 --> 00:02:05,967
keyboard players had high levels of mixed-handedness, whereas string players like cellists

28
00:02:05,967 --> 00:02:09,300
and violinists strongly favoured one hand.

29
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:14,340
Also those who started younger were more mixed-handed.

30
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:20,000
Matthews also reports studies of handedness in apes.

31
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,040
Apes get a large proportion of their food by 'fishing' ants from ant hills.

32
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:33,245
The studies show that apes, like humans, show handedness-though for them right-

33
00:02:33,245 --> 00:02:40,450
and left-handedness is about equal, whereas about 85% of humans are right-handed.

34
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:47,646
Studies showed that apes consistently using the same hand fished out 30% more ants

35
00:02:47,646 --> 00:02:50,730
than those varying between the two.

36
00:02:50,730 --> 00:03:00,000
Matthews started researching several different sports and found different types of handedness in each.

37
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,404
By the way, he uses 'handedness' to refer to the dominant side for feet

38
00:03:05,404 --> 00:03:07,720
and eyes as well as hands.

39
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:14,854
Anyway, his team measured the hand, feet and eyes of 2,611 players

40
00:03:14,854 --> 00:03:20,890
and found that there were really three main types of laterality:

41
00:03:20,890 --> 00:03:26,482
mixed-you work equally well on both sides-both hand and eye;

42
00:03:26,482 --> 00:03:34,312
single-you tend to favour one side but both hand and eye favour the same

43
00:03:34,312 --> 00:03:34,871
side;

44
00:03:34,871 --> 00:03:40,464
and cross-laterality-a player's hands and eyes favour only one side

45
00:03:40,464 --> 00:03:43,260
but they are opposite sides.

46
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:45,590
Let's start with hockey.

47
00:03:45,590 --> 00:03:50,312
Matthews found that it was best to be mixed-handed-this is

48
00:03:50,312 --> 00:03:58,339
because a hockey stick must be deployed in two directions-it would be a drawback to have hand

49
00:03:58,339 --> 00:04:00,700
or eye favouring one side.

50
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:09,231
An interesting finding is that mixed-handed hockey players were significantly more confident than their

51
00:04:09,231 --> 00:04:10,450
single-handed counterparts.

52
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:15,360
Things are slightly different in racket sports like tennis.

53
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:19,985
Here the important thing is to have the dominant hand

54
00:04:19,985 --> 00:04:22,760
and eye on the one side.

55
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:27,756
This means that there is a bigger area of vision on the side where

56
00:04:27,756 --> 00:04:29,540
most of the action occurs.

57
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:34,812
If a player is cross lateral the racket is invisible from the dominant eye

58
00:04:34,812 --> 00:04:40,084
for much of the swing, it means that they can only make corrections much

59
00:04:40,084 --> 00:04:40,461
later...

60
00:04:40,461 --> 00:04:43,850
and often the damage has been done by then.

61
00:04:43,850 --> 00:04:50,090
And moving to a rather different type of sport which involves large

62
00:04:50,090 --> 00:04:52,170
but precise movements gymnastics.

63
00:04:52,170 --> 00:04:56,840
It's been found that cross hand-eye favouring is best.

64
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:03,100
The predominant reason for this is because it aids balance which is of course

65
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:06,230
absolutely central to performance in this sport.
